 <paragraph index="205" node_type="writer">My own computer can’t be used for YOLOv5s training because of its lack of a GPU, but I can do the YOLOv5s training directly on the LIMO according to its characteristics.</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="207" node_type="writer">2-My Ubuntu version is too recent for ROS</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="208" node_type="writer">Indeed, we need Ubuntu 20.04 for ROS1.</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="210" node_type="writer">Solution:</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="211" node_type="writer">We will use Docker and work on it. We can directly work on the LIMO, but we choose to work on Docker to enable us to continue working outside the Lab.</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="217" node_type="writer">II- THE VIRTUAL ENVIRONMENT OF WORK(ROBOT_PROJECT)</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="218" node_type="writer">Since OpenCv as its own Python version, to avoid a conflict of Python with my original computer Python version I created a virtual environment for executing the OpenCV code.</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="220" node_type="writer"># ENTER the environment</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="221" node_type="writer">source ~/robot_project/bin/activate</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="222" node_type="writer">→ (robot_project) appears</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="224" node_type="writer"># EXIT the environment</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="225" node_type="writer">deactivate</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="226" node_type="writer">→ (robot_project) disappears</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="228" node_type="writer"># Re-enter anytime</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="229" node_type="writer">source ~/robot_project/bin/activate</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="230" node_type="writer">→ (robot_project) 	appears again</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="238" node_type="writer">III- LANGUAGES AND SOFTWARE INSTALLATION</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="240" node_type="writer">1-Python:</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="241" node_type="writer">We will use OpenCV, YOLOv5s, and ROS for the project, and each one of them needs:</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="242" node_type="writer">- OpenCV needs: Python 3.13</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="243" node_type="writer">- YOLOv5s needs: Python 3.11</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="244" node_type="writer">- ROS needs: Python 3.8</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="246" node_type="writer">Python version on computer:</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="247" node_type="writer">Unfortunately, this version only matches with OpenCV. 

Solution: </paragraph>
 <paragraph index="248" node_type="writer">We will install Python 3.11 for YOLOv5s and for ROS we don’t need because Python 3.8 Noetic is in Docker.
</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="249" node_type="writer">Python 3.11 installation: Sudo apt install -y python3.11 python3.11-venv python3.11-dev</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="251" node_type="writer">error during installation:</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="252" node_type="writer">This problem come from the absence of Python 3.11 in the default repository of Ubuntu 25.04 I have, so to continue the installation we need to add it.</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="254" node_type="writer">Installation by building Python 3.11 from source</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="255" node_type="writer">This is the most reliable method. Run each command one by one:</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="257" node_type="writer">1-Remove the broken deadsnakes repository</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="258" node_type="writer">sudo add-apt-repository --remove ppa:deadsnakes/ppa</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="259" node_type="writer">sudo apt update</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="260" node_type="writer">2- Install buil dependencies</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="261" node_type="writer">sudo apt install -y build-essential zlib1g-dev \</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="262" node_type="writer">libncurses5-dev libgdbm-dev libnss3-dev \</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="263" node_type="writer">libssl-dev libsqlite3-dev libreadline-dev \</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="264" node_type="writer">libffi-dev curl libbz2-dev</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="266" node_type="writer">3- Download Python 3.11 source code</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="267" node_type="writer">cd /tmp</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="268" node_type="writer">curl -O https://www.python.org/ftp/python/3.11.9/Python-3.11.9.tgz</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="269" node_type="writer">4- Extract the source code</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="270" node_type="writer">tar -xzf Python-3.11.9.tgz</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="271" node_type="writer">cd Python-3.11.9</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="272" node_type="writer">5- Configuration of the build</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="273" node_type="writer">./configure –enable-optimizations</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="274" node_type="writer">6- Compilation of Python 3.11</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="275" node_type="writer">make -j$(nproc)</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="276" node_type="writer">7- Installation of Python 3.11</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="277" node_type="writer">sudo make altinstall</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="278" node_type="writer">8- Verification of the installation</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="279" node_type="writer">python3.11 –version</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="280" node_type="writer">Result:</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="282" node_type="writer">9- Installation of pip for Python 3.11</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="283" node_type="writer">curl -sS https://bootstrap.pypa.io/get-pip.py | python3.11</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="284" node_type="writer">10-Verification of the pip</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="285" node_type="writer">python3.11 -m pip --version</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="286" node_type="writer">Result :</paragraph>
