 <paragraph index="306" node_type="writer">2-Docker and Ros</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="311" node_type="writer">Since my computer runs ubuntu 25, which isn’t compatible with ROS, we chose to run ROS in a Docker container.</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="313" node_type="writer">Docker installation:</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="315" node_type="writer">Once Docker is installed, we must restart the computer. If we don’t want to restart the computer, we must inform the computer the use Docker by entering “newgrp docker” in the terminal.</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="317" node_type="writer">The first thing we must do every time we turn on my computer is to launch all the container. We do it with the following command:</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="318" node_type="writer">- docker start -ai ros_kelly</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="322" node_type="writer">Steps to use ROS on Docker</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="323" node_type="writer">We need at least three terminals, and to configure them we follow the following commands:</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="325" node_type="writer">Terminal 1:  The master (the bain)</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="326" node_type="writer">This terminal manages the phonebook so the two other terminals can find each other.</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="327" node_type="writer">1- Enter the container: docker exec -it ros_kelly bash</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="328" node_type="writer">2- Sourcing ROS: source /opt/ros/noetic/setup.bash</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="329" node_type="writer">3- Turn on ROS tools or start the heart: roscore</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="331" node_type="writer">Terminal 2:  The listener (the subscriber)</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="332" node_type="writer">This terminal represents the robot logic (like a robot arm waiting for a command).</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="333" node_type="writer">1- enter the container: docker exec -it ros_kelly bash</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="334" node_type="writer">2- Sourcing ROS: source /opt/ros/noetic/setup.bash</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="335" node_type="writer">3- Listening to the channel: rostopic echo /chatter</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="337" node_type="writer">Now, the Terminal 2 is waiting for a command coming from the Terminal 3.</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="339" node_type="writer">Terminal 3:  the talker (the publisher)</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="340" node_type="writer">This terminal represents the sensors (like the LIMO PRO camera or LiDAR).</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="341" node_type="writer">1- Enter the container: docker exec -it ros_kelly bash</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="342" node_type="writer">2- Sourcing ROS: source /opt/ros/noetic/setup.bash</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="343" node_type="writer">3- Send a message: rostopic pub -r 1 /chatter std_msgs/String &quot;data: 'LIMO robot is ready'&quot;</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="345" node_type="writer">Once we enter the command 3, the message ‘LIMO robot is ready’ will be displayed in the 2nd terminal.</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="347" node_type="writer">

</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="350" node_type="writer">
3- Gazebo Installation</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="351" node_type="writer">commands to install Gazebo:</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="352" node_type="writer">apt install ros-noetic-gazebo-ros-pkgs ros-noetic-gazebo-ros-control</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="354" node_type="writer">After installing Gazebo, when I tap gazebo in the container nothing happens ; it is because gazebo is a graphical interface ans Docker is currently « blind », and has no way to send the video signal on my computer screen. Docker is trying to display an image but has no way to do it. To fix this we will bridge Docker ( the box) and the host Ubuntu 25.04.  To do it we must the follow the following steps :</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="356" node_type="writer">1- On the Ubuntu 25.05 ( out of Docker) :</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="357" node_type="writer">xhost +local:docker</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="358" node_type="writer">result :</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="359" node_type="writer">
</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="360" node_type="writer">
It means that the computer is ready to accept visual drawing from Docker container.</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="362" node_type="writer">2- We must now indicate to the Docker container where to send the drawing, to do this we must set DISPALY as environment variable to let the software know it can look at the host screen :</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="363" node_type="writer">export DISPLAY=:0</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="365" node_type="writer">Now we will try to test the graphical link again :</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="366" node_type="writer">- Let’s install a tiny test app : apt update &amp;&amp; apt install x11-apps -y</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="367" node_type="writer">- Run the clock : xclock</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="368" node_type="writer">result :
</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="372" node_type="writer">This means that even though the two part ( Ubuntu 25.04 and the container ) have the configurations needed, the tunnel that link the two has not be created. </paragraph>
 <paragraph index="375" node_type="writer">Solution :</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="376" node_type="writer">We will recreate a container but this time with a tunnel to send the drawing toward the host ( Ubuntu 25.04) screen.</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="378" node_type="writer"> Steps : </paragraph>
 <paragraph index="379" node_type="writer">1- First we must stop and remove the old container :</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="380" node_type="writer">docker stop ros_kelly</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="381" node_type="writer">docker rm ros_kelly</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="383" node_type="writer">2- Grant GPU permissions to docker from the host :</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="384" node_type="writer">xhost +local:docker</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="386" node_type="writer">2- Launch the new container with GPU support :</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="387" node_type="writer">docker run -it \</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="388" node_type="writer">    --name ros_kelly \</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="389" node_type="writer">    --env=&quot;DISPLAY=$DISPLAY&quot; \</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="390" node_type="writer">    --env=&quot;QT_X11_NO_MITSHM=1&quot; \</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="391" node_type="writer">    --volume=&quot;/tmp/.X11-unix:/tmp/.X11-unix:rw&quot; \</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="392" node_type="writer">    osrf/ros:noetic-desktop-full</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="394" node_type="writer">Test : </paragraph>
 <paragraph index="395" node_type="writer">- In the host : xhost +local:docker</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="396" node_type="writer">- Open a master terminal and configure it like above :
	1- Enter the container: docker exec -it ros_kelly bash</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="397" node_type="writer">	2- Sourcing ROS: source /opt/ros/noetic/setup.bash</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="398" node_type="writer">	3- Turn on ROS tools or start the heart: roscore
</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="399" node_type="writer">
-Open another terminal ( we can call him terminal 4) for send the display :</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="400" node_type="writer">	1- Enter the container: docker exec -it ros_kelly bash</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="401" node_type="writer">	2- Sourcing ROS: export DISPLAY=:0</paragraph>
 <paragraph index="402" node_type="writer">	3- Turn on ROS tools or start the heart:  gazebo.

